b5a7c0-71c0-543a-9a51-5b97dd34d5a9" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396808} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ae6b545-b77c-5d5b-9931-736b82549b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396815} {:answer/id #uuid "89424f11-45a5-55bd-b37e-893709471e0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396816} {:answer/id #uuid "2cbb751e-a325-5db5-b363-985130d0a919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396817} {:answer/id #uuid "975ab894-9921-5186-81fe-4c114ee83d5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396818} {:answer/id #uuid "6d9cbd1d-e481-5685-ac5d-c46ede74e55e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d9cbd1d-e481-5685-ac5d-c46ede74e55e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396819} :answer-field/id #uuid "12adf0fb-a2ca-581e-a5b3-29d3bd797f25" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396814} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "210f1777-cf42-569d-a917-65499a15c777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396821} {:answer/id #uuid "1b87a34c-7c82-5aee-b376-23612b63939e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396822} {:answer/id #uuid "49ad5f41-7687-51cb-ba4a-590a4318edc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b87a34c-7c82-5aee-b376-23612b63939e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396822} :answer-field/id #uuid "53fe5cf4-ec62-5c60-b7a4-8115f70289d6" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e15b51c9-b3ac-5fdf-bdd1-db7479e393a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-335438" :question/problem "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=3x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,$ then $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,3x=O(3x)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2x})=1$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=O(2x)$ as $x\\to 0$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,f=O(5x)$ as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from lines \n{{field-2}} and \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line {{field-4}}  and \n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x),g(x),h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation. \n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(k)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf+g=O(h+k)\\,\\text{and}\\,fg=O(hk)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property.\n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let us justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1},$ which states that\n\n$$\n3x=O({{3x}_{⏟}}_{f}).\n$$\n\nThis is true by $\\text{the reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we have\n\n$$\n3x=O(3x),\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\n-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=O(2x)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThus, by lines $\\text{L1 and L3}$ and $\\text{the sum property},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf=O(5x)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line  $\\text{L4}$   we have\n\n$$\nf=O(5x)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0,\n$$\n\nand by the $\\text{constant multiple property}$ with $c=5,$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(x)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffe4292f-7653-5486-975e-c206acae734e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of Big-O"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35c14a88-2f8e-50c7-a553-cce91615d84d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396826} {:answer/id #uuid "bffdbb57-4fd0-54cf-b6f9-dc38b7998642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396827} {:answer/id #uuid "84d83159-e6f7-5dcb-96ee-01f76997a673" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35c14a88-2f8e-50c7-a553-cce91615d84d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396826} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc543933-c02b-591f-8cf5-11706b38483b" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396825} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0171ac5-2979-51d5-8963-edfbcd9126a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396830} {:answer/id #uuid "6f3f8806-972d-5465-9195-f699121c2965" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396831} {:answer/id #uuid "3f5d818a-1c81-53fe-85eb-42fb5d104302" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396832} {:answer/id #uuid "7336c43b-083b-5aca-9d74-a1c4fbc23c0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396833} {:answer/id #uuid "25be9492-14c1-5151-bd2a-f48309b37689" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0171ac5-2979-51d5-8963-edfbcd9126a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396830} :answer-field/id #uuid "e018cb38-5370-53d0-b6a4-410327d4f73b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396829} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa92b838-82ae-5998-8890-3edccb9454f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396836} {:answer/id #uuid "9422130e-7d41-59c0-9c3b-e82a870fd3e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396837} {:answer/id #uuid "b10d5f73-5ec3-5ec2-a7e2-6bdd3b13d3dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396838} {:answer/id #uuid "fedcf2a1-58c2-5fe0-abf5-3ba83b146f86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396839} {:answer/id #uuid "ff395391-34ba-5fcb-a35c-97965c0d981e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9422130e-7d41-59c0-9c3b-e82a870fd3e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396837} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3bbb676-81e2-50e3-a3bb-87f4e8b01e41" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396835} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adcbfc07-80e8-5ea1-b84e-13f16a7e0b7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396842} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb130c3-0aff-5067-91a1-ee45706f1245" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396843} {:answer/id #uuid "8054b0b3-df38-5516-9370-a63b32150442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396844} {:answer/id #uuid "8f721fc4-0680-57ca-bbe6-1e1378545e6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396845} {:answer/id #uuid "b80f8558-60e8-5dc7-9392-0ec85bf1101f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b80f8558-60e8-5dc7-9392-0ec85bf1101f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396846} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b22cd9c-5cf4-532f-a1ca-970ec9a3d464" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396841} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "096d8579-05d7-55b7-ab38-419cac3562a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L2 and L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396848} {:answer/id #uuid "8ebff648-bb6f-5ff2-84af-8d82f7000bf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L1 and L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ebff648-bb6f-5ff2-84af-8d82f7000bf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L1 and L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396849} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d4d43f0-56f8-5977-b48a-2665291f0625" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac4293b7-4265-5467-bc7b-b86d5611daed" :question/math-academy-id "q-335445" :question/problem "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=2+{e}^{-x},$ then $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,f=O(2+{e}^{-x})$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}=0$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,{e}^{-x}=O(2)$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,f=O(2)$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O(1)$  \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from lines  \n{{field-2}}  \nand  \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line  \n{{field-4}}  \nand  \n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x),g(x),h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation. \n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(k)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf+g=O(h+k)\\,\\text{and}\\,fg=O(hk)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property. \n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen \n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1},$ which states that\n\n$$\nf=O({{2+{e}^{-x}}_{⏟}}_{f}),\n$$\n\nand this is true by the $\\text{reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(2+{e}^{-x}),\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-x}=O(2).\n$$\n\nThus, by lines   $\\text{L1 and L3}$ and the  $\\text{absorption property},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf=O(2).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L4},$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(2)\n$$\n\nand by the $\\text{constant multiple property}$ with $c=2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(1).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding Definitions of Stability Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddec3d21-d9a8-5b05-8b8b-bf5789fceefb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "arbitrarily close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396852} {:answer/id #uuid "2ddafa6a-7da8-507f-81ed-08fd9d2db8ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "far from" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddec3d21-d9a8-5b05-8b8b-bf5789fceefb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "arbitrarily close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396852} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fae1d8c-606a-5bd4-a555-2a631ad7f437" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396851} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3edfa3a9-5202-50e5-bd72-0e10a5d47289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396855} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec4c355-4b8a-576a-a899-56bcc23c083e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Some" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ec4c355-4b8a-576a-a899-56bcc23c083e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Some" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396856} :answer-field/id #uuid "06642795-f3b2-5a1d-8756-9f9c0fba8344" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396854} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57360b16-4b38-50df-85d1-531bb626e68a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "remain close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396858} {:answer/id #uuid "0c02ae13-acc9-5b84-b898-d987d50404ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "eventually move away from" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c02ae13-acc9-5b84-b898-d987d50404ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "eventually move away from" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396859} :answer-field/id #uuid "58ab2b4c-0421-57e3-9439-37e1ee616b4e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4f31534-294a-5c66-bb7a-bbd9c8ed5e89" :question/math-academy-id "q-335460" :question/problem "Select the correct options in the statement below that describe an **unstable** equilibrium point.{{field-1}} solutions that start\n{{field-2}}\nthe equilibrium\n{{field-3}}\nthe equilibrium." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n$\\text{Some}$ solutions that start $\\text{arbitrarily close to}$ the equilibrium $\\text{eventually move away from}$ the equilibrium."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding Definitions of Stability Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7acacba1-6f69-50e7-9864-e82f875f3e4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396862} {:answer/id #uuid "31ed6079-395e-5ee2-ba11-4ffbcddbe9aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "away from" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396863} {:answer/id #uuid "b985aed1-7358-5e7b-ab66-d0a4361f12c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "exactly at" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7acacba1-6f69-50e7-9864-e82f875f3e4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396862} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd666725-4037-50ad-9322-132e6584f76a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396861} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f797104a-b169-5e09-845d-5f807e277b2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "approach and move away from" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396866} {:answer/id #uuid "1f4783cf-2a80-559e-b115-4da84ec3ff7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "remain close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396867} {:answer/id #uuid "ab06307b-561e-561b-bc5b-ec96a6bebff5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "eventually move away from" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f4783cf-2a80-559e-b115-4da84ec3ff7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "remain close to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396867} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d867b79-f420-59a0-b427-5d7e13137929" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396865} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20612cc0-84ee-5b88-a26f-470820172fa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "may not converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396870} {:answer/id #uuid "671730d5-989d-55ab-a270-ae825f4fbb30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "always converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396871} {:answer/id #uuid "9898c13e-f508-5911-928d-68e0d683195d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "never converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "671730d5-989d-55ab-a270-ae825f4fbb30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "always converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396871} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd4a8bc6-57d0-51df-b61d-7a76918f0898" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ab9c96d-932f-5272-8662-18e0a52dc3ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-335463" :question/problem "Select the correct options in the statement below that describe an **asymptotically stable** equilibrium point.The solutions that start sufficiently\n{{field-1}}\nthe equilibrium\n{{field-2}}\nit for all future time, and\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\nThe solutions that start sufficiently $\\text{close to}$ the equilibrium $\\text{remain close to}$ it for all future time, and $\\text{always converge to the equilibrium as }t\\to \\infty .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding Definitions of Stability Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3abae5d3-02ce-535c-bb22-07a9d6832cf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "reach" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396875} {:answer/id #uuid "64a6e9e1-8142-5c95-9768-8b45c83b