/ref 17592186397028} {:answer/id #uuid "9075b9b5-02ae-5817-9f6e-b66c5225cfc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397029} {:answer/id #uuid "25c082de-d2be-5ef1-b6ea-07729e875ea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a8ade83-a027-534c-bd4f-84aa0200d2d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397026} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bedb308-447b-574c-bd8b-26248f559b30" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a01f9794-773d-5e12-a888-6dc684788a68" :question/math-academy-id "q-337376" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=5.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{3x})}^{′}-3(u{e}^{3x}) & =5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x}-3u{e}^{3x} & =5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}} & =5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =5 \\\\ {u}^{′} & =5{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =5{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 5{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =-\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{3x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{3x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ac34a18-053d-5eab-8c2c-bd0f15658186" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c107f37e-7439-51cd-8125-7b533838b1a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\int {e}^{-x}(2x-5)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397033} {:answer/id #uuid "7d799668-ca03-5716-985f-696822b47c93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\int {e}^{-x}(2x+5)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397034} {:answer/id #uuid "8713ba55-0219-5ca2-aa75-e93b1a2aef1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}\\int {e}^{-x}(5-2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397035} {:answer/id #uuid "03d06967-2bd8-5ad1-ad41-704cc3be560a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}\\int {e}^{x}(2x-5)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c107f37e-7439-51cd-8125-7b533838b1a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\int {e}^{-x}(2x-5)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397033} :answer-field/id #uuid "008636f6-a3d9-555d-ad4c-a5efd04935ce" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397032} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a43a251-2319-5a92-9184-9e3033842d12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397038} {:answer/id #uuid "82de528b-a7d0-591d-bfd6-8981068dfdca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397039} {:answer/id #uuid "e73f10dd-6b3a-5dd0-bdff-640976047082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397040} {:answer/id #uuid "6459c483-0244-5e04-914d-7557e9e9be0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397041} {:answer/id #uuid "cb57fa42-6e17-53a6-b42d-1f4e39f65e1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6459c483-0244-5e04-914d-7557e9e9be0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397041} :answer-field/id #uuid "46425785-cb0c-5916-9859-40a15b476f37" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397037} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fc81de6-5f49-50eb-962c-7815ddf3c293" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{-x}(5-2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397044} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5ccb87-cc4f-5977-af9d-3e72e6140cb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{-x}(2x-5)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397045} {:answer/id #uuid "f883e0ee-9ac6-5e1d-8eda-78ecb99c56ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{-x}(2x+5)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397046} {:answer/id #uuid "cfba429b-73d7-56fd-a05b-13890a45950f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{x}(2x-5)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e5ccb87-cc4f-5977-af9d-3e72e6140cb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{-x}(2x-5)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397045} :answer-field/id #uuid "80779cba-994a-51af-b9ca-eb4077594bf4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc82baf7-6e13-5137-9b62-5ced3f36541b" :question/math-academy-id "q-337395" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y=2x-5.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{x})}^{′}-(u{e}^{x}) & =2x-5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x}-u{e}^{x} & =2x-5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{x}+\\cancel{u{e}^{x}}-\\cancel{u{e}^{x}} & =2x-5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{x} & =2x-5 \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-x}(2x-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-x}(2x-5) \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-x}(2x-5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{-x}(2x-5)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-x}(2x-5)\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\int {e}^{-x}(2x-5)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397048 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using VOP When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "544ef45e-9801-59af-90c5-d2e5923d0da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397050} {:answer/id #uuid "7e87c929-0475-5c78-9a67-dac2d411868c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397051} {:answer/id #uuid "a0435832-f556-5180-9a87-eb33c09ea5ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397052} {:answer/id #uuid "19d94403-f572-5514-880b-e5648b1c55b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 175921