math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397063} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd362fc-1231-5202-af1f-85e205bfff31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397064} {:answer/id #uuid "bcae8dee-48c8-50be-9a9d-dd37d0130528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397065} {:answer/id #uuid "59738da9-7945-56e4-9208-a9ec7560aaa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcae8dee-48c8-50be-9a9d-dd37d0130528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397065} :answer-field/id #uuid "6be5a4a4-18b9-5519-8e71-87176119df5c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60c74d98-62e0-535f-ae2c-bd22a02ff3d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-337466" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y={e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-4x})}^{′}+4(u{e}^{-4x}) & ={e}^{-4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-4u{e}^{-4x}+4u{e}^{-4x} & ={e}^{-4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{-4x}}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{-4x}} & ={e}^{-4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x} & ={e}^{-4x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using VOP When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e403ce47-06c5-5e9b-ab8e-462b81d03f09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397069} {:answer/id #uuid "11a32a7f-7b9c-504f-b1d3-2e76cef86def" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397070} {:answer/id #uuid "96b52573-5826-593c-8432-290b29b2304f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397071} {:answer/id #uuid "0ff820c1-181b-5e4b-acbc-aa742b9a3b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397072} {:answer/id #uuid "1ea5bfac-c767-5b9d-ab68-68b54a6358b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397073} {:answer/id #uuid "4222ee29-c478-5b04-aa00-976fed70c609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4222ee29-c478-5b04-aa00-976fed70c609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397074} :answer-field/id #uuid "abaac5ec-4860-5fab-abd5-9e1c5fafe889" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397068} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79ff23f2-915d-5d8b-9114-3779b44c89bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397076} {:answer/id #uuid "a584cd0d-27c6-531c-970d-493932778886" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397077} {:answer/id #uuid "38447498-29d3-5175-a4df-a73a990ee625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397078} {:answer/id #uuid "0124f381-e063-50ed-b5a1-3c3f9da3ad9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397079} {:answer/id #uuid "603df059-0fa2-51f8-bca9-c190d6e5b9ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0124f381-e063-50ed-b5a1-3c3f9da3ad9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397079} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2f973d5-c3c3-591c-86ce-78dde5c8e25a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397075} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7665d218-e973-5ccf-a112-1b3cee61a4b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397082} {:answer/id #uuid "2a5fbc5d-982a-5e37-a3dc-84a876e64fc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397083} {:answer/id #uuid "b7470fb6-388e-532f-839c-4263ebaddc37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397084} {:answer/id #uuid "9448a899-f5a3-5f84-b46b-10313439f92c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7665d218-e973-5ccf-a112-1b3cee61a4b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397082} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d0b526f-0a36-505c-bd42-a28d844cfb3c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34254624-9c90-5339-836d-8f0fcd78b2d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-337469" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+7y={e}^{-7x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+7{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-7x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-7x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-7x})}^{′}+7(u{e}^{-7x}) & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-7x}-7u{e}^{-7x}+7u{e}^{-7x} & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-7x}-\\cancel{7u{e}^{-7x}}+\\cancel{7u{e}^{-7x}} & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-7x} & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-7x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-7x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{-7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397086 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using VOP When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06d45bf0-b0d2-526d-9ee8-966bee2bf82f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x{e}^{5x}-{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397088} {:answer/id #uuid "f899948d-7d57-564f-9c17-dc1a4e8c1e4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5x}-x{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397089} {:answer/id #uuid "cd83b01b-26af-5c69-90f0-dea06d731251" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5x}-\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397090} {:answer/id #uuid "ba369324-b632-52dd-b087-db8abad2c3bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x{e}^{5x}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba369324-b632-52dd-b087-db8abad2c3bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x{e}^{5x}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397091} :answer-field/id #uuid "02bdb63b-0f09-5614-88a9-63938cc89b6d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397087} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5bc26fc-760a-5855-9195-3473708b71ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-{e}^{-10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397093} {:answer/id #uuid "80570deb-15eb-5c3a-beac-50b739cab35b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{-10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397094} {:answer/id #uuid "32a1c91a-9d5b-5cd5-ac23-19ae0f28b96e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397095} {:answer/id #uuid "f12ce1c7-e215-555b-8e8d-d49be3f26f0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-x{e}^{-10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32a1c91a-9d5b-5cd5-ac23-19ae0f28b96e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397095} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddc85c1c-2146-58a2-8708-42bac8c22805" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397092} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cbf3f92-2ab9-584c-9c0e-47d30d63d2a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397098} {:answer/id #uuid "d41e52db-7e65-579d-9821-9e969bdbaf23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397099} {:answer/id #uuid "104a2da1-9f11-510a-9e86-88dce1ddf0c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397100} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc049b9-0f4d-5a90-aa1e-01e88bb54d2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{15x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397101} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3e4f63-8e52-5102-b305-3e0c04fc80b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "104a2da1-9f11-510a-9e86-88dce1ddf0c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397100} :answer-field/id #uuid "23391fc0-0d97-510f-b68a-65d0d3b75416" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5bf23900-2f3d-53c0-9448-69b2b01a2a2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-337488" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y=10\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{5x})}^{′}-5(u{e}^{5x}) & =10\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+5u{e}^{5x}-5u{e}^{5x} & =10\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}}-\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}} & =10\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x} & =10\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =10{e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x=\\frac{{e}^{5x}+{e}^{-5x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =10{e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\  & =10{e}^{-5x}(\\frac{{e}^{5x}+{e}^{-5x}}{2}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{-5x}({e}^{5x}+{e}^{-5x}) \\\\  & =5+5{e}^{-10x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =5+5{e}^{-10x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 5+5{e}^{-10x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =5x-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-10x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{5x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{5x} \\\\  & =5x{e}^{5x}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90930e15-0827-5e0b-82cf-98263fc756d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b5ec8b6-d55b-55cc-aa94-056dd87936ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397105} {:answer/id #uuid "d8f3b959-aec4-56a5-ac52-93392b485d16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b5ec8b6-d55b-55cc-aa94-056dd87936ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397105} :answer-field/id #uuid "01b3194e-1c5b-5f63-8af7-cad12a568ef1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397104} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e0647b-2813-5eba-b947-eeac3bfb20f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397108} {:answer/id #uuid "f34ae965-4585-556a-b037-c112e1a82720" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f34ae965-4585-556a-b037-c112e1a82720" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397109} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ac21c56-a1ed-58af-8f2a-f8f3a643de10" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397107} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fdd2df26-875b-5cf8-8e35-d22dc66dd099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397111} {:answer/id #uuid "61df961a-7458-5bdd-bf21-6f1ffc7be0e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61df961a-7458-5bdd-bf21-6f1ffc7be0e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397112} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8930119-dde1-5c7f-9dc6-b7a8c29faee5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8da06f22-6a38-578e-b590-3e2d4a9025f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-338004" :question/problem "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.$\\,$ The equation $\\,\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{″}-2x{y}^{′}+y=0$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}=x$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}-xy+4x=0$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{″}-2x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}=x\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=x,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-xy+4x=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-xy=-4x.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=-4x,$ which is different from zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90930e15-0827-5e0b-82cf-98263fc756d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8224d08c-b6d5-59c1-a3e9-c4005d2ce278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397115} {:answer/id #uuid "51641394-cf83-58e5-aa34-474bd8353286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51641394-cf83-58e5-aa34-474bd8353286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397116} :answer-field/id #uuid "84f1c5b4-e827-513e-b3a4-9b5e916f4d90" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397114} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c982b65b-e870-508e-bf5e-ddcdb473e18a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397118} {:answer/id #uuid "7497ecc8-2fd5-55ef-ad5f-c1e4eba3b91f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7497ecc8-2fd5-55ef-ad5f-c1e4eba3b91f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397119} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbe235fa-bcf8-5953-9422-9db01e330fa5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397117} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c21f26fa-303e-5e74-bcc7-27f756e59fef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397121} {:answer/id #uuid "81be49a9-a2c9-51ce-9466-8f23516f81e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c21f26fa-303e-5e74-bcc7-27f756e59fef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397121} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee6a8920-f14a-5940-b560-371ca67535b3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e8b12875-ae8a-5464-b1d4-8be3ec87c5f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-338082" :question/problem "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+2y=x$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-6y=0$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}-(x+2)y+5x=0$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=x\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=x,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-6y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-(x+2)y+5x=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-(x+2)y=-5x.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=-5x,$ which is different from zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152581 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af8cf798-4327-5f5b-82ab-f622f20ccc38" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous Boundary Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdcbfc02-0951-530a-bdd6-e4d3b384e4bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Robin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397124} {:answer/id #uuid "3f18baa1-7086-526c-b930-04b3cbe22feb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Neumann" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397125} {:answer/id #uuid "1819390b-ed10-5cad-95ce-fcd58864ce6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dirichlet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdcbfc02-0951-530a-bdd6-e4d3b384e4bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Robin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397124} :answer-field/id #uuid "20af0270-925d-5adc-bd31-842158df2ebb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397123} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "215a0a6c-ad0f-54a4-b9ce-0e55ccae84c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397128} {:answer/id #uuid "e3518d61-df59-5386-b3ca-172e84278fc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3518d61-df59-5386-b3ca-172e84278fc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397129} :answer-field/id #uuid "edc7f057-557e-5798-8bd0-23cc616cd262" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397127} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a97f21e-a71d-5e5e-9343-1f163db58ab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397131} {:answer/id #uuid "37849ebb-b4e1-594a-a35e-046d0b945819" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a97f21e-a71d-5e5e-9343-1f163db58ab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397131} :answer-field/id #uuid "53d409c4-1129-5429-b0b7-a65bc0e5e62a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397130} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e141a51c-1bad-5df3-b98a-e9adf08222fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397134} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c5252f-c30d-56e5-9e4e-8b841dfc1553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7c5252f-c30d-56e5-9e4e-8b841dfc1553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397135} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f2d4158-46ad-5262-82de-55f5e7b1262a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b711ebb4-4d84-521d-8e27-4cb308558d9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-338516" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+{x}^{2}{y}^{′}(x)+y(x)=2x,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi \n$$\n\n$$\n2y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(\\pi )+2{y}^{′}(\\pi )=0\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\,$ The governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., ${\\gamma }_{1}={\\gamma }_{2}=0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f≡0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+{x}^{2}{y}^{′}(x)+y(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x)=2x≢0,$ the equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\n2y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(\\pi )+2{y}^{′}(\\pi )=0.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\text{Robin}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are zero for both, they are $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is inhomogeneous, yet the boundary conditions are homogeneous, the BVP is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af8cf798-4327-5f5b-82ab-f622f20ccc38" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous Boundary Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ae358f3-d85b-5a1d-acc8-17c991405ff1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Robin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397137} {:answer/id #uuid "0b2c9783-f7e5-5797-b9f1-9f95b4b6673c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Neumann" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397138} {:answer/id #uuid "bdec2590-f44d-5d16-8f47-5c405afcd805" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dirichlet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b2c9783-f7e5-5797-b9f1-9f95b4b6673c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Neumann" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397138} :answer-field/id #uuid "70ebcc26-25af-598a-97e1-499e2407aeb0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397136} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe776fd0-8801-585d-a7c4-73bb386abf6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397141} {:answer/id #uuid "04b12e10-d19c-5892-a70b-db9c711fc435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe776fd0-8801-585d-a7c4-73bb386abf6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397141} :answer-field/id #uuid "18073122-bf85-5077-973b-f8f6df4708b0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397140} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d23b2ad9-78b8-5e2c-978b-06f7f0b9daca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397144} {:answer/id #uuid "0fde711e-fce0-537b-85be-fb90e2b76123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fde711e-fce0-537b-85be-fb90e2b76123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397145} :answer-field/id #uuid "f19326da-14c5-5131-a8ff-fcda659769b1" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397143} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f02b63af-f692-59a6-a607-4bbd33881ab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397147} {:answer/id #uuid "bc949215-7547-54ff-8dd9-dd5a5c2df74a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc949215-7547-54ff-8dd9-dd5a5c2df74a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397148} :answer-field/id #uuid "99ab627e-d079-50a2-8b6c-0a78cf9cf5f3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc19be1d-a106-5173-ab6a-0a0d552a59ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-338646" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{′}(x)+3y(x)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 2\n$$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=-1\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\,$ The governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., ${\\gamma }_{1}={\\gamma }_{2}=0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f≡0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{′}(x)+3y(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x)≡0,$ the equation is $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=-1.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\text{Neumann}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are not zero for both, they are $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is homogeneous, yet the boundary conditions are inhomogeneous, the BVP is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229727 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "532a73e6-e026-57c9-9bad-57cd2f75b6df" :knowledge-point/title "Parity Recognition and Series Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "777c0bde-5c50-58aa-bcd9-af554298f662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397150} {:answer/id #uuid "18bbb808-067a-5c73-a58a-7bbbccd30b52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18bbb808-067a-5c73-a58a-7bbbccd30b52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397151} :answer-field/id #uuid "00839b4e-f62d-5523-b0a0-cd44b3f38d1f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397149} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4046271d-3273-5e3f-a757-9112f2fda282" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "sine terms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397153} {:answer/id #uuid "3f94849a-be66-556b-b362-390c2fd77a3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "cosine terms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f94849a-be66-556b-b362-390c2fd77a3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "cosine terms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397154} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2ef1e3c-f0e2-50af-90e8-066404b32b3d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7903a61-d4e3-5a7b-991a-79f6efafb8b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-338682" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Select the correct options below.$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the \n{{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=-f(x),$ we have that $f$ is odd.\n\nFor Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${a}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{odd}$ and the $\\text{cosine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d9576e0-3be5-5d05-8cc8-c3ecde496b46" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Solutions of Homogeneous BVPs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b159e62b-c689-5e20-8226-00fb7f02cbc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has the unique solution $y≡0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397157} {:answer/id #uuid "296d3c7d-583d-5979-b5c1-8a501ca9c153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397158} {:answer/id #uuid "45559ca1-ecc3-53ab-b9cb-735c0349e5ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b159e62b-c689-5e20-8226-00fb7f02cbc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has the unique solution $y≡0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397157} :answer-field/id #uuid "e847dcfa-8d4c-527b-a527-764fa7af066c" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397156} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c42e4b6e-5657-51bb-8a20-e55e05bf0b4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397161} {:answer/id #uuid "8e6ce6c5-93bd-58b1-9fb8-00392e5ae9bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397162} {:answer/id #uuid "c741b2ff-ab7f-50e9-8510-a9245c14a314" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397163} {:answer/id #uuid "c0522330-3e52-5790-9a75-e3f8ff7f852d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e6ce6c5-93bd-58b1-9fb8-00392e5ae9bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397162} :answer-field/id #uuid "45104fed-d5b1-5f34-94f6-9880f2f08e51" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397160} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f1122a1-93f6-5c64-9f87-7969988c17b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det(A)\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397166} {:answer/id #uuid "b91b465b-5b4d-556f-8377-2a1cf46f49c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det(A)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f1122a1-93f6-5c64-9f87-7969988c17b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det(A)\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397166} :answer-field/id #uuid "51dfa5bc-f977-5d9b-ba50-23229e7fd4f8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397165} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d279e548-5266-5f27-9d03-c8398d64cae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397169} {:answer/id #uuid "54744ac7-fbfd-50c3-8251-2c3e6a450573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397170} {:answer/id #uuid "b2774d8c-38d7-522f-be26-a346562dc684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397171} {:answer/id #uuid "43570723-fc37-5b8a-8a4b-ada76825e92e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397172} {:answer/id #uuid "74cbe6be-bb9e-5d5a-9836-2608cbc207da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}+1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54744ac7-fbfd-50c3-8251-2c3e6a450573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397170} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb5e74c1-7ef2-5956-a46b-09ebf9d20450" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2dbc0eba-24a7-50e0-964f-4c8949e5821f" :question/math-academy-id "q-338686" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-2y=0,\\,y(0)=y(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem has a unique solution if and only if {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The fundamental solutions to this BVP are {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, we have $det(A)=$ {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, this BVP {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)=0,\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Dirichlet boundary conditions. So, setting ${\\alpha }_{i}=1,{\\beta }_{i}=0,a=0,$ and $b=1,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-m-2 & =0 \\\\ (m-2)(m+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=2$ and $m=-1,$ and so the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}\\text{ and }{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ {e}^{2} & {e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={e}^{-1}-{e}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}-{e}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{3}}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)\\ne 0$ we conclude that this BVP $\\text{has the unique solution }y≡0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af8cf798-4327-5f5b-82ab-f622f20ccc38" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous Boundary Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed15164b-b252-57a7-9aea-30435b9a3865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397176} {:answer/id #uuid "cddd9a34-b737-58d1-9afb-cdb77c0c26f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed15164b-b252-57a7-9aea-30435b9a3865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397176} :answer-field/id #uuid "502c80ef-3dec-548c-927b-2e9b14bb965a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397175} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f24f87a8-01c4-5e7c-89a1-5d0f6d2ca765" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Robin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397179} {:answer/id #uuid "36bf4826-046d-5dea-84ce-588b7abe7e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Neumann" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397180} {:answer/id #uuid "4de81ab8-eb50-5141-bc5b-5482b95979d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dirichlet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f24f87a8-01c4-5e7c-89a1-5d0f6d2ca765" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Robin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397179} :answer-field/id #uuid "731a8ac7-b834-5f3c-9988-da7fada8b09f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397178} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53642858-4ce5-5373-905a-d8916de650d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397183} {:answer/id #uuid "9a8f292a-ee48-5ba5-9da3-8c6c59413e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53642858-4ce5-5373-905a-d8916de650d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397183} :answer-field/id #uuid "844987f3-1e3f-53c7-ae11-937954d8e862" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397182} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "967be8db-6c8c-5176-a37a-cfbb2d0acb70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397186} {:answer/id #uuid "3558fe78-12f6-5e58-8639-b31e8f48b34e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "967be8db-6c8c-5176-a37a-cfbb2d0acb70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397186} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7c7990c-99a3-522c-9968-be9df4628906" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8670636a-35fe-5cb8-86c2-aff8f53ead1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-338697" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}(x)+y(x)=0,\\,1\\le x\\le 2\n$$\n\n$$\ny(1)+{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,5y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\,$ The governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., ${\\gamma }_{1}={\\gamma }_{2}=0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f≡0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}(x)+y(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x)≡0,$ the equation is $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\ny(1)+{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,5y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\text{Robin}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are zero for both, they are $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is homogeneous, and the boundary conditions are homogeneous, the BVP is $\\text{homogeneous}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af8cf798-4327-5f5b-82ab-f622f20ccc38" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous Boundary Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73c0e445-a378-5434-8482-d18eb5704c6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Robin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397190} {:answer/id #uuid "7306602c-11b5-5992-8e61-45db9cbc8