lect} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397196} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31af7fa4-4513-5aef-83fe-a1d8f2b8b2e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397200} {:answer/id #uuid "2595a937-6145-59d8-b9db-727ca3b3098d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2595a937-6145-59d8-b9db-727ca3b3098d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397201} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3fa453d-7107-5ec3-9427-f1bb56e42551" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "748763ae-3cf4-5f3d-aa38-02f8044e2446" :question/math-academy-id "q-338700" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,{y}^{′}(x)+y(x)=0,\\,-1\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(-1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\,$ The governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., ${\\gamma }_{1}={\\gamma }_{2}=0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f≡0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,{y}^{′}(x)+y(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x)≡0,$ the equation is $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(-1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\text{Neumann}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are not zero for both, they are $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is homogeneous, yet the boundary conditions are inhomogeneous, the BVP is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af8cf798-4327-5f5b-82ab-f622f20ccc38" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous Boundary Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81cc9abd-b067-56fe-9533-831b282c548c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Robin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397204} {:answer/id #uuid "79219c09-ee73-5582-b7f0-3c92d3e24434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Neumann" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397205} {:answer/id #uuid "2f432c12-01eb-5ab3-b66e-a07f7961fb29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dirichlet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79219c09-ee73-5582-b7f0-3c92d3e24434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Neumann" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397205} :answer-field/id #uuid "64e29f55-8029-5108-a581-698602c30477" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397203} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "135741f7-fe86-5292-a33a-499ba6d19968" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397208} {:answer/id #uuid "311509b9-9db7-52b4-ab56-4b0fdb8f104f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "311509b9-9db7-52b4-ab56-4b0fdb8f104f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397209} :answer-field/id #uuid "5827196b-5d11-58a5-8374-869b17b516ae" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397207} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aca9f9a4-a5c0-5fb4-b089-3a7f47aa2f2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397211} {:answer/id #uuid "35658182-a940-5623-a87d-fd2e05811467" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aca9f9a4-a5c0-5fb4-b089-3a7f47aa2f2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397211} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d9bbfef-5d8a-5eb4-afd5-bff174a7e808" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397210} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2636ba5a-474e-5a5d-9cb3-c2f759834dd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397214} {:answer/id #uuid "da6fc2f4-1dc3-536a-a5eb-cd6619ddf024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da6fc2f4-1dc3-536a-a5eb-cd6619ddf024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397215} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5c9b5ab-10e3-5417-9498-0531c5e83157" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b12a4da-b770-5d48-8104-f0ea5dff8e44" :question/math-academy-id "q-338713" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{′}(x)-2y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi \n$$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(\\pi )=0\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\,$ The governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., ${\\gamma }_{1}={\\gamma }_{2}=0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f≡0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{′}(x)-2y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x≢0,$ the equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(\\pi )=0.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\text{Neumann}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are zero for both, they are $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is inhomogeneous, yet the boundary conditions are homogeneous, the BVP is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397216 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba4d320f-517e-5dc6-9c6d-02d1c2488472" :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Eigenfunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6333f256-57a4-524f-882c-85ce13ed4d52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{x}$ and $Cx{e}^{-x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397218} {:answer/id #uuid "bdff980e-244b-5407-8c35-36869326e1f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-\\frac{3}{2}x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397219} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9fce80-afaa-5cf2-a342-0dd3523e12a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397220} {:answer/id #uuid "bc315ebe-9716-5917-b194-2a4a36b373d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{2x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397221} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce119a8-474d-516f-b714-f6645bb4943e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{\\frac{3}{2}x}$ and $Cx{e}^{-\\frac{3}{2}x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdff980e-244b-5407-8c35-36869326e1f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-\\frac{3}{2}x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397219} :answer-field/id #uuid "6451528f-6193-5fce-847d-24fbb635dd58" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397217} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f942b92-7058-551e-856d-94bbeff34573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397224} {:answer/id #uuid "e417753c-3dcf-5b7e-8c3e-55d245a9b3a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397225} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff25083-915f-5352-b85b-65678f3833df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397226} {:answer/id #uuid "e1eb5523-b928-553e-8031-34e6f2d343f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{3}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397227} {:answer/id #uuid "182b3678-ac7b-5812-986d-8764905ed502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{3}{14}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "182b3678-ac7b-5812-986d-8764905ed502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{3}{14}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397228} :answer-field/id #uuid "81e0c9b5-cbc2-598f-97c0-6c5a9eeadf86" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba070694-f520-5989-9c36-fcafe8653bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-338837" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-14\\lambda {y}^{′}+49{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(2)+{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=0$ and $y(2)+{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-14\\lambda m+49{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-7\\lambda )(m-7\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=7\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{7\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{7\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{7\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{7\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA+B\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=Bx{e}^{7\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=B{e}^{7\\lambda x}+7B\\lambda x{e}^{7\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2B{e}^{14\\lambda }+B{e}^{14\\lambda }+14B\\lambda {e}^{14\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 3B{e}^{14\\lambda }+14B\\lambda {e}^{14\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{14\\lambda }+\\frac{14}{3}B\\lambda {e}^{14\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{14\\lambda }(1+\\frac{14}{3}\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require $B\\ne 0.$ Thus, since ${e}^{14\\lambda }>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{3}{14}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding eigenfunction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =Bx{e}^{7\\lambda x} \\\\  & =Bx{e}^{-\\frac{3}{2}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397229 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba4d320f-517e-5dc6-9c6d-02d1c2488472" :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Eigenfunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bbb88d2-d106-5a48-a491-8a1865d27c49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{7}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397231} {:answer/id #uuid "67f9752d-2717-5fd3-89ed-ddffa71fb940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397232} {:answer/id #uuid "a197a422-5c87-5907-bb4d-6cb21111b01d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397233} {:answer/id #uuid "e1402155-85cf-54ae-8dae-41b284721aaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{7}{18}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397234} {:answer/id #uuid "de1e54e4-c76b-5bbc-878c-a545826e8820" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1402155-85cf-54ae-8dae-41b284721aaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{7}{18}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397234} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c538818-2826-5c63-ba40-2de95adb895c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397230} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "140b4847-4a5b-5d7f-86de-35bc43b0ad69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{7x/6}$ and $Cx{e}^{-7x/6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397237} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9a82c9-1222-5042-83d2-13a77db7ac2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{x}$ and $Cx{e}^{-x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397238} {:answer/id #uuid "8a10ebeb-c0e2-55fd-a474-2902f9e065b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397239} {:answer/id #uuid "ef701c60-7e0e-59df-91ed-7707b3fcd28a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-7x/6}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397240} {:answer/id #uuid "9efb216e-ffb8-5585-b11d-72d7a980688a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-7x/3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef701c60-7e0e-59df-91ed-7707b3fcd28a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-7x/6}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397240} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3f0e357-b57a-5092-8107-004d7ace2a2f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4fbda52c-e557-5901-ad9a-c542b15bd0a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-338853" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-6\\lambda {y}^{′}+9{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,2y(2)+3{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=0$ and $2y(2)+3{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-6\\lambda m+9{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-3\\lambda )(m-3\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=3\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{3\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{3\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA+B\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=Bx{e}^{3\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=B{e}^{3\\lambda x}+3B\\lambda x{e}^{3\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $2y(2)+3{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2B{e}^{6\\lambda })+3(B{e}^{6\\lambda }+6B\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ 4B{e}^{6\\lambda }+3B{e}^{6\\lambda }+18B\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{6\\lambda }(7+18\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require $B\\ne 0.$ Thus, since ${e}^{6\\lambda }>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{7}{18}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding eigenfunction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =Bx{e}^{3\\lambda x} \\\\  & =Bx{e}^{-7x/6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96494014-7289-548b-9749-2eb6336c9cef" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Solutions of Inhomogeneous BVPs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57953917-9b55-5619-a644-205d5986c778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has the unique solution $y≡0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397244} {:answer/id #uuid "84309482-f526-5187-af7f-dc14b214756c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397245} {:answer/id #uuid "4c30858b-6958-5b31-94ac-a6c2b49da2dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57953917-9b55-5619-a644-205d5986c778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has the unique solution $y≡0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397244} :answer-field/id #uuid "469938c6-f220-5660-b177-dff8d9814679" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397243} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9a08f35-f8cd-5a90-bedd-518473486f75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397248} {:answer/id #uuid "d88de683-917f-5c51-a866-9f444be17076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397249} {:answer/id #uuid "19b0377b-6071-5f48-bceb-b57e0d0432e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has a unique solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397250} {:answer/id #uuid "ed9dc3a2-e8c2-5c1a-ae6f-935009d158f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has infinitely many solutions or no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19b0377b-6071-5f48-bceb-b57e0d0432e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "has a unique solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397250} :answer-field/id #uuid "7184a2d1-142e-5a80-b6b3-2020f6f8c935" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397247} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74a65952-2694-54eb-b16e-b141038d04a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397253} {:answer/id #uuid "13f225ac-2005-50c6-8990-1374897e301c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397254} {:answer/id #uuid "01a78aaf-52d8-5e47-ac82-4d80dbcff436" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397255} {:answer/id #uuid "5b62f57e-bba8-5a70-81e8-84dc47d97b58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397256} {:answer/id #uuid "faa5f024-9c77-51f7-b98c-9d941600d936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}+1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b62f57e-bba8-5a70-81e8-84dc47d97b58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397256} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd092bf8-3470-5e3b-adb1-e164fc3a35de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8eb48225-775a-576c-bf42-f55c3f321d8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-338878" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-2y=4x,\\,y(0)=1,\\,y(1)=3,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ be fundamental solutions of the form $y={e}^{mx}$ to the corresponding homogeneous BVP, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below **without calculating the solution** to this BVP.\n\n$\\,$ The **absolute value** of $det(A)$ is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the corresponding homogeneous BVP {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, our inhomogeneous BVP {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP)\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $P,Q,$ and $f$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nThe inhomogeneous BVP has at most one solution if and only if the associated homogeneous BVP has only the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Specifically:\n\n- If the homogeneous BVP has the unique solution $y≡0,$ then the inhomogeneous problem has a unique solution.\n\n- If the homogeneous BVP has (infinitely many) nontrivial solutions, then the inhomogeneous problem either has infinitely many solutions or no solution.\n\nTo classify the solutions of the corresponding homogeneous BVP, we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe homogeneous BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ In other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the homogeneous BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Dirichlet boundary conditions. So, setting ${\\alpha }_{i}=1,{\\beta }_{i}=0,a=0,$ and $b=1,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-m-2 & =0 \\\\ (m-2)(m+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=2$ and $m=-1,$ and so the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ {e}^{2} & {e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(A)| & =|{e}^{-1}-{e}^{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{e}-{e}^{2}| \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)\\ne 0,$ the corresponding homogeneous BVP $\\text{has the unique solution }y≡0.$\n\nTherefore, since the corresponding homogeneous BVP has only the trivial solution $y≡0$, our inhomogeneous BVP $\\text{has a unique solution}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba4d320f-517e-5dc6-9c6d-02d1c2488472" :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Eigenfunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "877ab039-605d-549e-8622-6cae0d465fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397260} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf99903-ee3b-5e01-a931-22d951facaab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397261} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a527a7-acdb-51b7-9ade-49877be410a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{x}$ and $C{e}^{-x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397262} {:answer/id #uuid "479d00a1-2920-59e5-b016-b0576cc21641" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "877ab039-605d-549e-8622-6cae0d465fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397260} :answer-field/id #uuid "36ec000b-c994-55b9-b093-1daaa5f63bf9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397259} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50817ef9-0b55-5b6b-94aa-573ea49716ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397265} {:answer/id #uuid "42571170-ae49-58a1-a447-73aed3d500ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{10}$ and $\\frac{1}{20}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397266} {:answer/id #uuid "219f78d1-948d-5466-8f67-a7b5b558a098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{20}$ and $\\frac{1}{10}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397267} {:answer/id #uuid "2f1b1192-6a7f-53e2-8bde-b8b0685bd05c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42571170-ae49-58a1-a447-73aed3d500ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{10}$ and $\\frac{1}{20}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397266} :answer-field/id #uuid "75123b29-65f0-56eb-bdd5-9f239c208387" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e67017cf-eb62-5988-9350-d0872682f901" :question/math-academy-id "q-338905" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-20\\lambda {y}^{′}+100{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-20\\lambda m+100{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-10\\lambda )(m-10\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=10\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{10\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{10\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{10\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{10\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =10A\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda x}+B{e}^{10\\lambda x}+10B\\lambda x{e}^{10\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10A\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda }+B{e}^{10\\lambda }+10B\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 10A\\lambda +B+10B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (10\\lambda )A+(1+10\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{20\\lambda }+2B{e}^{20\\lambda }+2(10A\\lambda {e}^{20\\lambda }+B{e}^{20\\lambda }+20B\\lambda {e}^{20\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+2(10A\\lambda +B+20B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+20A\\lambda +2B+40B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+20\\lambda )A+(4+40\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+20\\lambda )A+4(1+10\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(10\\lambda )A+(1+10\\lambda )B=0 \\\\ (1+20\\lambda )A+4(1+10\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}10\\lambda  & 1+10\\lambda  \\\\ 1+20\\lambda  & 4(1+10\\lambda )\\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\lambda \\cdot 4(1+10\\lambda )-(1+10\\lambda )(1+20\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )[40\\lambda -(1+20\\lambda )] & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )[40\\lambda -1-20\\lambda ] & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )(20\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{10}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{20}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{10}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{10{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{10{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(10\\lambda )A+(1+10\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+(1-1)B=0\n$$\n\n \nwhich gives $A=0,$ and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{20},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{10{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{10{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(10\\lambda )A+(1+10\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{3}{2}B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-3B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-3B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-3) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $Bx{e}^{-x}\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397269 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "472cbb79-e22b-50d2-a997-93130ff66c22" :knowledge-point/title "Trigonometric Eigenfunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34c15024-ad26-5f05-9256-bec2be1ec08d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397271} {:answer/id #uuid "0f13f8cc-8f5b-59e1-9e1b-8571f15b04e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397272} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0e24c0-3ab7-5f6f-99fb-9147b822880e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397273} {:answer/id #uuid "0adf033e-d27a-587a-b356-01b5a700380c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397274} {:answer/id #uuid "28b42bdc-4e58-542b-8a98-ce465846e44e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3n}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34c15024-ad26-5f05-9256-bec2be1ec08d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397271} :answer-field/id #uuid "00e92f05-552a-5096-91d9-24d9da00fdbd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397270} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d149f557-f909-5ed0-be99-174185a4e256" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{\\lambda }_{n}x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397277} {:answer/id #uuid "5923f52e-15f1-5a9c-b5a9-fa0804ebc1e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3{\\lambda }_{n}x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397278} {:answer/id #uuid "15092485-4594-5a18-ac64-9c22f0bd52ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397279} {:answer/id #uuid "531f6fe0-e7a7-5f58-9025-95838ba779ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397280} {:answer/id #uuid "5907806c-b6ca-50c7-955f-3897edc07f12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397281} {:answer/id #uuid "86e0f9bf-00a1-5204-9647-0bc6ec485936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86e0f9bf-00a1-5204-9647-0bc6ec485936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397282} :answer-field/id #uuid "57b058f9-2857-5bd3-9ddc-d9ffd448c64e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "958d5083-ae6b-5fcf-874a-f964110a9edf" :question/math-academy-id "q-338907" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,y(0)=y(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=y(\\pi )=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+9{\\lambda }^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 3\\text{i}\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\lambda x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\lambda x)\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\lambda x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(\\pi )=0$ yields\n\n$$\nB\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\lambda \\pi )=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n3\\lambda \\pi =n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{n}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3{\\lambda }_{n}x),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"}]}
